findings.description:
Both deleteWebhook failure paths log `err.getMessage()` / `compErr.getMessage()` raw:

  Line 215: log.warn("Telegram deleteWebhook failed during Disconnect; proceeding to local update: {}", err.getMessage());
  Line 161: log.warn("Compensating deleteWebhook failed during Connect rollback: {}", compErr.getMessage());

These errors originate from TelegramApiClient.deleteWebhook, which calls WebClient against URI template `/bot{token}/deleteWebhook`. When the failure is a non-transient transport error not filtered by `Retry.filter(isTransient)` (e.g. SSL handshake failure, DNS resolution failure, unsupported-protocol, or any other Reactor-Netty / Spring WebClient exception that does not match WebClientResponseException(5xx) / IOException / TimeoutException / ReadTimeoutException), the exception's `getMessage()` typically contains the full request URI — which embeds the plaintext bot token in the path segment. TelegramApiClient.scrubTokens is only applied inside the client's own `toAppException` WARN line; it is NOT applied to exceptions that propagate to the caller. AppException factory exceptions produced inside the client carry safe canned messages, but raw WebClient/Netty exceptions (the very ones that bypass the retry filter) carry the URI.

This violates R1 ("Bot token leak through logs or API responses") and AC17 ("No log line in this file carries the token field"). The whole file otherwise scrupulously avoids logging the token; this single primitive (`err.getMessage()` on a deleteWebhook error) defeats that posture.

The same concern does NOT apply to the Redis log sites (lines 252, 262) because Redis exception messages do not contain the bot token.

findings.recommendation:
Run any error message captured from telegramApiClient through a token scrubber before logging. Either expose TelegramApiClient.scrubTokens as `public static` and call it here, or replicate the same regex locally and apply it to `compErr.getMessage()` and `err.getMessage()` before passing to log.warn. Example:

  private static final java.util.regex.Pattern TOKEN_PATTERN =
          java.util.regex.Pattern.compile("\\d{1,20}:[A-Za-z0-9_-]{30,50}");
  private static String scrub(String msg) {
      return msg == null ? null : TOKEN_PATTERN.matcher(msg).replaceAll("[REDACTED_TOKEN]");
  }
  ...
  log.warn("Telegram deleteWebhook failed during Disconnect; proceeding to local update: {}", scrub(err.getMessage()));
  log.warn("Compensating deleteWebhook failed during Connect rollback: {}", scrub(compErr.getMessage()));

Add a regression test asserting that a WebClientRequestException whose message contains a token-shaped substring is scrubbed at the BotService log call site. The cleanest path is to promote `TelegramApiClient.scrubTokens` from package-private static to public static and reuse it — single source of truth.
